- How would you monitor and alert on a spike in invalid message rate?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests experience designing scalable, governed data platforms with multi-tenant isolation, data contracts, and end-to-end lineage on GCP. It also probes handling late-arriving data, PII masking, and per-tenant rollback plans.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant isolation in BigQuery with Authorized Views
- Data contracts and lineage via Data Catalog
- Ingestion-time PII masking with DLP
- Event-time processing and late-arrivals in Dataflow
- Cross-region replication and immutable audit logs
- Schema evolution and per-tenant rollback strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: ingest path with DLP masking before storage
const ingest = (record) => {
  const masked = dlp.maskPII(record);
  pubsub.publish(masked);
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test schema evolution across 300 tenants without downtime?
- What metrics and alerting would you implement for cross-region replication health?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes design for per-tenant data quality and schema evolution in a large-scale streaming pipeline, focusing on data catalog lineage, backward-compatible changes, drift detection, and rollback – all essential for production data platforms.

## Key Concepts

- Streaming data quality checks (null density, ranges, referential integrity)
- Per-tenant isolation in storage and compute
- Schema evolution with backward compatibility
- End-to-end lineage via Data Catalog
- Drift detection and tenant-specific rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal validator against a registry schema (conceptual)
function validateRecord(record, schema) {
  // assume 'schema' is loaded from Data Catalog
  const valid = ajv.validate(schema, record);
  if (!valid) throw new Error('Validation error: ' + ajv.errorsText());
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to test schema drift across tenants and automate remediation?
- How to handle late-arriving data and replays without breaking lineage?
- How to enforce tenant isolation at storage and compute layers?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design complex, scalable pipelines with dynamic schemas, per-tenant isolation, and robust rollback. Evaluates integration of Pub/Sub, Dataflow, BigQuery, Data Catalog, and access controls, plus testing and lineage practices.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic schema versioning and canonicalization
- Per-tenant access controls (Authorized Views)
- Schema evolution and backfill strategies
- End-to-end data lineage and contracts

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo Beam map to canonical schema (high level)
PCollection<Event> events = pubsub.readEvents("projects/.../topics/...");
events.apply(ParDo.of(new DoFn<EncryptedEvent, CanonicalEvent>() {
  @ProcessElement
  public void process(@Element EncryptedEvent e, OutputReceiver<CanonicalEvent> out) {
    CanonicalEvent c = mapToCanonical(e); // uses tenant-specific schema_version
    out.output(c);
  }
}));
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test schema drift without production impact?
- How would you implement data contracts in Data Catalog and enforce them at ingestion?

## Why This Is Asked
This angle explores multi-tenant isolation, masking, and rollback in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- Dataflow (Beam) streaming pipelines
- Pub/Sub for ingestion
- Per-tenant BigQuery datasets
- Data Loss Prevention masking in ingest
- Idempotent writes with MERGE
- Dead-letter queues and raw data archiving
- 24h rollback via replay from Cloud Storage
- Observability through Dataflow metrics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Dataflow masking sketch (pseudo)
class MaskPII {
  process(element) {
    if (element.email) element.email = '*****@*****.com';
    if (element.phone) element.phone = '**********';
    return element;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test data quality across tenants? 
- How would you enforce per-tenant quotas in BigQuery and Dataflow?
